## Commands

```bash
uv sync --extra dev          # install, including dev tools
cp .env.example .env         # first time only
uv run austial serve         # dev server on :8000 with reload
uv run austial g resource <name>   # scaffold a full CRUD feature module
uv run ruff check . --fix && uv run ruff format .
uv run mypy src tests
uv run pytest -q
```

**Definition of done:** `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `mypy src tests`
and `pytest` all pass. Never report work as finished without running them.

## Layout

```
src/
├── main.py              # AustialFactory.create(AppModule) + global pipes/filters
├── app/
│   ├── app_module.py    # root @Module: imports every feature module
│   ├── app_controller.py
│   ├── app_service.py
│   └── modules/<snake_name>/        # one folder per feature
│       ├── <name>_module.py
│       ├── <name>_controller.py
│       ├── <name>_service.py
│       ├── entities/<name>_entity.py
│       └── dto/create_<name>_dto.py, update_<name>_dto.py
├── shared/                          # used by more than one module
│   ├── guards/ services/ pipes/ interceptors/ filters/ decorators/ dto/
└── templates/                       # non-Python assets
    └── email/
tests/unit/, tests/e2e/      # files named *_spec.py
```

- One folder per feature. File names use underscore suffixes
  (`cats_controller.py`), never dots.
- Code used by two or more modules goes in `src/shared/<kind>/`, not in a feature
  folder another module imports from. Feature folders never import each other's internals;
  they go through the other module's exported provider.
- Imports are absolute from the project root: `from src.app.modules.cats...`,
  `from src.shared.guards...`.
- Prefer `uv run austial g resource <name>` over hand-writing a feature. It
  also patches `src/app/app_module.py`. Use `--shared` for cross-cutting pieces:
  `uv run austial g guard roles --shared`, `uv run austial g service mailer --shared`.
- More folders are fine when the app needs them (`src/config/`, `src/database/`,
  `src/jobs/`, `src/events/`); keep the same one-folder-per-concern rule.

## The mental model

Request pipeline, in order: **guards -> pipes -> interceptors -> handler ->
exception filters**. Middleware runs before all of it.

1. **Everything must be registered.** A controller goes in a module's
   `controllers=[...]`, a provider in `providers=[...]`, and the module in
   an `imports=[...]` chain that reaches `AppModule`. A missing registration
   shows up as a missing route or a DI error, not an import error.
2. **Dependency injection is by `__init__` type hint.** Providers are
   singletons (`Scope.DEFAULT`) unless marked `Scope.TRANSIENT`. There is no
   request scope. Never instantiate a service yourself with `Foo()`.
3. **Repositories** are injected as
   `repo: Repository[Cat] = InjectRepository(Cat)`, and the owning module
   needs `OrmModule.for_feature([Cat])`. The root needs one
   `OrmModule.for_root(...)`.
4. **Request data** uses marker defaults: `Body()`, `Param()`, `Query()`,
   `Headers()`. DTOs are plain pydantic `BaseModel`s. Route paths use `:id`
   and `:key*` (catch-all that also matches `/`), not `{id}`.
5. **Errors:** raise Austial exceptions (`NotFoundException`,
   `BadRequestException`, `ConflictException`, ...). Do not hand-build error
   responses. The global `AllExceptionsFilter` renders
   `statusCode/message/error/timestamp/path`.
6. **Guards are the security boundary.** Every non-public route must be
   covered by `AuthGuard` (and `RolesGuard` / `TenantGuard` where relevant).
   Mark intentionally open routes with `@Public()`.

